            <ArticleTitle>Examining the effect of three low-intensity pediatric obesity interventions: a pilot randomized controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1367-74</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0009922814541803</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Primary care is an ideal setting to treat pediatric obesity. Effective, low-intensity (≤25 contact hours over 6 months) interventions that reduce standardized body mass index (z-BMI) and can be delivered by primary care providers are needed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This pilot randomized controlled trial investigated the effect of 3 low-intensity (≤25 contact hours over 6 months) pediatric obesity treatments on z-BMI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-two families (children 8.0 ± 1.8 years, z-BMI of 2.34 ± 0.48) were randomized into 1 of 3, 6-month, low-intensity conditions: newsletter (N), newsletter and growth monitoring (N + GM), or newsletter and growth monitoring plus family-based behavioral counseling (N + GM + BC). Anthropometrics and child eating and leisure-time behaviors were measured.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Mixed-factor analyses of variance found a significant (P &lt; .05) main effect of time for z-BMI and servings per day of sugar sweetened beverages, with both decreasing over time.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Low-intensity obesity treatments can reduce z-BMI and may be more feasible in primary care.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2014.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
